  {
    "pkattachmentId": 36590,
    "bookid": 34,
    "chapterId": 305,
    "fileId": 10018,
    "type": "Table",
    "diagnosis": "",
    "title": "<p>Pattern-based classification of HPV-associated endocervical adenocarcinoma</p>",
    "legend": "",
    "source": "",
    "copyright": "Reprinted with permission from Roma AA, Diaz De Vivar A, Park KJ, Alvarado-Cabrero I, Rasty G, Chanona-Vilchis JG, et al. Invasive endocervical adenocarcinoma: a new pattern-based classification system with important clinical significance. Am J Surg Pathol. 2015 May;39(5):667-72. https://journals.lww.com/ajsp/Abstract/2015/05000/Invasive_Endocervical_Adenocarcinoma__A_New.11.aspx",
    "tablehtml": "<table class=\"MsoTableGridLight\" style=\"background: #DAEEF3; border-collapse: collapse; border: none;\" border=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\">\r\n<tbody>\r\n<tr style=\"page-break-inside: avoid; height: 10.2pt;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 626.45pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 10.2pt;\" valign=\"top\" width=\"708\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"page-break-after: avoid;\"><strong>Pattern&nbsp;A (non-destructive invasion)</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"page-break-inside: avoid; height: 17.2pt;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 626.45pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 17.2pt;\" valign=\"top\" width=\"708\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span>Well-demarcated glands with rounded contours</p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span>No lymphovascular invasion</p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span>Complex intraglandular growth acceptable (i.e. cribriform growth, papillae)</p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span>Lack of solid growth (i.e. architecturally well to moderately differentiated)</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"page-break-inside: avoid; height: 10.2pt;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 626.45pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 10.2pt;\" valign=\"top\" width=\"708\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"page-break-after: avoid;\"><strong>Pattern&nbsp;B (early&nbsp;/ focally destructive invasion)</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"page-break-inside: avoid; height: 17.2pt;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 626.45pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 17.2pt;\" valign=\"top\" width=\"708\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span>Individual or small groups of tumour cells, separated from the rounded glands; focally desmoplastic or inflamed stroma</p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span>Foci may be single, multiple, or linear at base of tumour</p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span>Lymphovascular invasion +/&minus;</p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span>Lack of solid growth (i.e. architecturally well to moderately differentiated)</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"page-break-inside: avoid; height: 10.2pt;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 626.45pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 10.2pt;\" valign=\"top\" width=\"708\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"page-break-after: avoid;\"><strong>Pattern&nbsp;C (diffusely destructive invasion)</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"page-break-inside: avoid; height: 17.2pt;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 626.45pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 17.2pt;\" valign=\"top\" width=\"708\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span>Diffusely infiltrative glands with associated extensive desmoplastic response</p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span>Glands often angulated or with canalicular pattern, with interspersed open glands</p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span>Confluent growth filling a 4&times;&nbsp;field (5&nbsp;mm): glands, papillae (stroma only within papillae), or mucin lakes</p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span>Solid, poorly differentiated component (architecturally high-grade); nuclear grade is disregarded</p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span>Lymphovascular invasion +/&minus;</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n</tbody>\r\n</table>",
    "imageUrl": null,
    "thumbnail": null
  },
  {
    "pkattachmentId": 36617,
    "bookid": 34,
    "chapterId": 305,
    "fileId": 15346,
    "type": "Table",
    "diagnosis": "",
    "title": "<p>Histological types of HPV-associated adenocarcinoma of the uterine cervix</p>",
    "legend": "",
    "source": "No source citation required - This box was constructed by the IARC technical editor based on the section text provided by the authors.",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "<table class=\"MsoTableGridLight\" style=\"width: 703.3pt; background: #DAEEF3; border-collapse: collapse; border: none;\" border=\"0\" width=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\">\r\n<tbody>\r\n<tr style=\"page-break-inside: avoid; height: 10.2pt;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 703.3pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 10.2pt;\" valign=\"top\" width=\"938\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"page-break-after: avoid;\"><strong>Usual type:</strong></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"page-break-after: avoid;\">This type accounts for ~75% of all endocervical adenocarcinomas <span class=\"Ref-PMID\">{29135516; 12207781}</span>. Cells with mucinous cytoplasm constitute only 0&ndash;50% of the tumour. Papillary (including villoglandular) and micropapillary growth can be observed, mimicking serous carcinoma architecturally but not cytologically.</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"page-break-inside: avoid; height: 17.2pt;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 703.3pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 17.2pt;\" valign=\"top\" width=\"938\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span><em>Villoglandular variant:</em> a rare lesion characterized by exophytic papillary growth <span class=\"Ref-PMID\">{8418074}</span>. Papillae are lined by columnar epithelium of usual type (most often) with mild atypia. Luminal borders are smooth. Invasion of the underlying cervical stroma is absent or minimal.</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"page-break-inside: avoid; height: 10.2pt;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 703.3pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 10.2pt;\" valign=\"top\" width=\"938\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>Mucinous type:</strong></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">This type accounts for ~10% of all endocervical adenocarcinomas <span class=\"Ref-PMID\">{29135516; 30679193}</span>. There is intracytoplasmic mucin in &ge;&nbsp;50% of cells, typically with a minor component of usual adenocarcinoma. This type is subdivided into the following variants:</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"page-break-inside: avoid; height: 17.2pt;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 703.3pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 17.2pt;\" valign=\"top\" width=\"938\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span><em>Mucinous NOS adenocarcinoma:</em> mucinous cytoplasm resembling normal endocervix with pale-purple staining on H&amp;E <span class=\"Ref-PMID\">{30679193}</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span><em>Intestinal adenocarcinoma:</em> goblet cell and/or enteroendocrine cell differentiation representing &ge;&nbsp;50% of the tumour <span class=\"Ref-PMID\">{18156982}</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span><em>Signet-ring cell adenocarcinoma:</em> loose non-cohesive round cells with a mucinous vacuole displacing the nucleus, representing &ge;&nbsp;50% of the tumour</p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span><em>Stratified mucin-producing carcinoma:</em> often associated with adjacent stratified mucin-producing intraepithelial lesion, composed of invasive nests of stratified epithelium with intracytoplasmic mucin; most are moderately or poorly differentiated <span class=\"Ref-PMID\">{26523540; 27237368}</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n</tbody>\r\n</table>",
    "imageUrl": null,
    "thumbnail": null
  },
  {
    "pkattachmentId": 36666,
    "bookid": 34,
    "chapterId": 305,
